  "transcription": {
    "transcript": "Today we are discussing stratified sampling. In stratified sampling, a population is divided into groups called strata. Researchers then select a random sample independently from each stratum. A sampling weight represents how many population members a simplified unit represents. It equals the inverse of that unit's probability of selection. For example, if a student has a one in 10 chance of being selected, their sampling weight is 10. Stratification can improve precision when individuals within each stratum are similar.",
